/// ============================ TRIPWIRE ==================================
///
/// THE TWO `WRONG_` TESTS BELOW PIN BEHAVIOR docs/name-resolution.md SAYS IS
/// WRONG. They pass today because the compiler is wrong today.
///
/// IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE ONE STARTED FAILING: that is the intended outcome,
/// and it means someone landed the fix. Do NOT delete the assertion to get
/// back to green. Move the case to a `compile_fail` project with the right
/// `expect.diagnostic`, in the same change that made it an error -- which is
/// exactly what happened to this file's visibility half on 2026-08-05; see
/// below.
///
/// Same trust-loop reasoning as tests/tests/projects/known_fail_canary: a
/// defect that nothing in CI observes is how this subsystem regressed for
/// years (§7's opening premise).
///
/// ---- WHAT LEFT, AND WHEN ----------------------------------------------
///
/// The cross-module `private` call that used to head this file is GONE: the
/// visibility gate landed on 2026-08-05 and it is now an E0353 error, pinned
/// through all three of its binding doors at
/// tests/tests/projects/visibility_gate. The flip was performed exactly as
/// the instruction above directs -- the case moved, it was not weakened.
///
/// What unblocked it was provenance. The gate had been built and reverted on
/// 2026-08-04 because its "does the use site live in the declaring module?"
/// exemption asked `current_module_ns()`, the AMBIENT CURSOR, which points at
/// whatever module is being processed rather than at the module that wrote
/// the call. The exemption now asks `module_ns_sym_of_file(span.file)`.
///
/// The §5.1 half below is untouched and still open.
///
/// ========================================================================
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionTripwire::TripwireTest;

import std::core::result;
import std::test::error;
import std::test::assert;

import ResolutionTripwire::Vault;

/// CONTROL for the gate that landed, and it stays HERE rather than moving to
/// the compile_fail project with the defect: this one has to keep RUNNING.
/// `visibility_gate` can only assert that a name is absent from a build's
/// diagnostics, which would still hold if public cross-module calls had been
/// broken some other way. This executes one and checks the value.
![test]
function CONTROL_a_public_function_is_callable_from_another_module()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    expect_eq(ResolutionTripwire::Vault::door(), 1)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

// ---- KNOWN DEFECT: a bare name resolves with NOTHING in scope (§5.1) ----
//
// §5.1: "The first segment resolves in scope. There is no substring
// matching, no suffix matching, and no dependence on what other modules
// happen to exist in the program."
//
// `ResolutionTripwire::Orphan` has no `import` statement at all, and names
// `Crate` in two signatures. `Crate` is declared only by
// `ResolutionTripwire::Depot`. Nothing binds it in Orphan's scope, so under
// §5.1 both signatures must be an E0203 "unknown type" -- and instead they
// resolve, because cascade step 5 (the GLOBAL LEAF INDEX, types/arena.cryo)
// answers from the whole program without consulting scope at all.
//
// MEASURED 2026-08-05, by deleting the equivalent import from
// tests/projects/generic_name_collision and diffing CRYO_LEAF_AUDIT:
// removing one import moved exactly 6 resolutions onto the leaf index --
// 3 logged with a home module set, 3 logged with an EMPTY use-site, the
// scope-less sites the §5.2 keystone exists to fix. Nothing else in the
// counter moved.
//
// This is §8.2a's root cause reduced to a project small enough to reason
// about: previously it was only observable as an aggregate over a full
// compiler build. It is the reason the leaf index cannot simply be deleted
// -- 2,538 answers in the compiler's own build depend on it, and every one
// of them is a name that was never in scope.
//
// THIS FLIPPED, AND BOTH ARMS WENT. The reachability gate (E0240) landed;
// `Orphan`'s bare `Crate` is now rejected through BOTH doors, so both cases
// moved to tests/tests/projects/namespace_gate as a compile_fail. They were
// moved, not weakened, exactly as the visibility half was -- `Orphan` itself
// now lives there, which is why this project no longer declares it.
//
// One thing did NOT come out clean, and namespace_gate records it: the
// free-function arm reports E0240 with the declaring module and an import
// suggestion, while the static-template arm reports E0233 ("no matching
// static method"). Scope is enforced on both -- the annotation fails to
// resolve and the template is rejected -- but the static path names the
// symptom instead of the cause. That is a diagnostic defect, not a hole in
// the gate, and it is pinned there rather than here.
//
// What survives below is the CONTROL half: the qualified path must keep
// reaching the same type, or a "fix" that made the arms above fail did so by
// breaking qualified paths rather than by enforcing scope.

/// CONTROL. `Depot` is imported nowhere by `Orphan`, but a module that DOES
/// spell the path in full must keep working -- otherwise a fix that made the
/// tripwires above fail did so by breaking qualified paths rather than by
/// enforcing scope.
![test]
function CONTROL_a_qualified_path_still_reaches_the_same_type()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut c: ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> =
        ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> { v: 25 };
    expect_eq(c.unwrap(), 25 as i64)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}
